### Stack
The stack is a region of memory that stores local variables and function call information. 

Stack Characteristics
Advantages
    Fast allocation/deallocation
    Automatic cleanup
    Cache friendly
Limitations
    Small size
    Cannot control lifetime beyond scope

Each function call creates a stack frame containing:
    Local variables
    Return address
    Function parameters

### Heap
Heap memory is manually managed.

Used when:
    Size is unknown at compile time
    Objects need longer lifetime
    Large memory allocation required

Difference Between Stack vs Heap
| Feature       | Stack       | Heap                  |
| ------------- | ----------- | --------------------- |
| Allocation    | Automatic   | Manual                |
| Speed         | Fast        | Slower                |
| Size          | Limited     | Large                 |
| Lifetime      | Scope-based | Programmer controlled |
| Fragmentation | No          | Possible              |


### Why RAII Is Powerful
    It gives:
    automatic cleanup
    exception safety
    no manual delete/free
    deterministic destruction
    fewer leaks

### Most Important Modern RAII Classes
| Resource       | RAII Type         |
| -------------- | ----------------- |
| Dynamic memory | `std::unique_ptr` |
| Shared memory  | `std::shared_ptr` |
| Dynamic arrays | `std::vector`     |
| Strings        | `std::string`     |
| File handling  | `std::ifstream`   |
| Mutex locking  | `std::lock_guard` |


### One-Line Summary
    RAII is the C++ technique of managing resources through object lifetime and automatic destruction.

### How would you detect memory leaks?
    1. Use tools
        Linux: Valgrind
        QNX
        Clang/GCC: AddressSanitizer
    2. Use IDE/debugger tools
    3. Code review patterns
    4. Prefer RAII to avoid leaks